content:
The dark part visible on the cuboid consists of:

1. two vertically stacked front cubes in the third column;
2. a black extension at the lower back/right, visible on the right side of the cuboid;
3. one further cube hidden immediately behind the lower front black cube.

So the dark piece must be the 3-dimensional “screw” shape: a chain of four cubes that turns twice in two perpendicular directions. This rules out B and D (which contain a straight run of three cubes), and C (which is planar in the form shown).

That leaves A and E, which are mirror images. In the cuboid, tracing the visible dark cubes from the upper front cube down, then back, then to the right gives the handedness of shape E; shape A would be its mirror image and cannot be obtained by rotation alone.

Thus the possible dark shape is

\[
\boxed{\text{E}}
\]
